chatgpt.text:
Debug it as a pipeline problem:

MOOSDB variable exists → app subscribes → OnNewMail receives it → state updates → Iterate runs → Notify publishes ODOMETRY_DIST → uXMS subscribes to ODOMETRY_DIST

Work through that chain in order.

1. First confirm the app is actually running

In the terminal where you launched the mission, check that your app appears in the process list / appcast output. If using pAntler, make sure your .moos file has something like:

moos
ProcessConfig = pOdometry
{
  AppTick   = 4
  CommsTick = 4
}

Then check with:

Bash
uXMS DB_CLIENTS

or in uMAC:

Bash
scope DB_CLIENTS

You should see your app listed as a MOOSDB client. If it is not there, the app never connected, so OnNewMail(), Iterate(), and Notify() are irrelevant until launch/config is fixed.

2. Check whether the input variables exist in MOOSDB

A pOdometry-style app usually computes distance from position updates, often something like:

C++
NAV_X
NAV_Y

or maybe:

C++
NAV_LAT
NAV_LONG

Start by watching the variables your app expects:

Bash
uXMS NAV_X NAV_Y

If they are not changing, your app has no position data to integrate. You can test manually with:

Bash
uPokeDB NAV_X=0 NAV_Y=0
uPokeDB NAV_X=10 NAV_Y=0

Then watch whether your app reacts.

3. Verify RegisterVariables() / subscriptions

Your app must explicitly subscribe to the variables it needs. In a typical MOOS app, this is done in OnStartUp() or a helper like RegisterVariables():

C++
bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  RegisterVariables();
  return true;
}

void Odometry::RegisterVariables()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}

Common mistakes:

C++
Register("NAV_X", 0);
Register("NAVY", 0);        // typo: should be NAV_Y
Register("nav_x", 0);       // MOOS variable names are case-sensitive
Register("NAV_X", "0");    // wrong type if your API expects double for interval

Also make sure RegisterVariables() is actually called. A surprisingly common bug is writing the function correctly but never invoking it.

4. Add temporary debug prints inside OnNewMail()

OnNewMail() should be the first place you verify that the app is receiving messages.

Add something like:

C++
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  MOOSMSG_LIST::iterator p;
  for(p = NewMail.begin(); p != NewMail.end(); p++) {
    CMOOSMsg &msg = *p;
    std::string key = msg.GetKey();

    std::cout << "Received mail: " << key << std::endl;

    if(key == "NAV_X") {
      m_current_x = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_x = true;
      std::cout << "NAV_X = " << m_current_x << std::endl;
    }
    else if(key == "NAV_Y") {
      m_current_y = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_y = true;
      std::cout << "NAV_Y = " << m_current_y << std::endl;
    }
  }

  return true;
}

Then relaunch the app and poke values:

Bash
uPokeDB NAV_X=1 NAV_Y=1
uPokeDB NAV_X=2 NAV_Y=1

If you do not see Received mail: NAV_X, the problem is subscription, variable spelling, app connection, or MOOSDB targeting.

If you do see the input variables, then OnNewMail() is working and the bug is probably in your state update, Iterate(), or Notify().

5. Check that Iterate() is running

Iterate() runs at the frequency set by AppTick.

For example:

moos
AppTick = 4

means Iterate() should run about 4 times per second.

Add:

C++
bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  std::cout << "Iterate running" << std::endl;

  return true;
}

If you never see this print, check:

moos
AppTick = 4

and make sure it is inside the correct ProcessConfig block for your app.

Bad:

moos
ProcessConfig = SomeOtherApp
{
  AppTick = 4
}

Good:

moos
ProcessConfig = pOdometry
{
  AppTick = 4
  CommsTick = 4
}

Also make sure your app name in the .moos file matches the binary/process name you launched.

6. Check the odometry logic inside Iterate()

A typical pattern is:

C++
bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  if(!m_got_x || !m_got_y)
    return true;

  if(!m_first_reading) {
    double dx = m_current_x - m_previous_x;
    double dy = m_current_y - m_previous_y;
    double delta = hypot(dx, dy);

    m_total_distance += delta;
  }

  m_previous_x = m_current_x;
  m_previous_y = m_current_y;
  m_first_reading = false;

  Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);

  return true;
}

Common bugs:

C++
if(m_first_reading)
  return true;

without ever setting m_first_reading = false.

Or:

C++
double total_distance = 0;

inside Iterate(), which resets the distance every tick. It should be a member variable like:

C++
double m_total_distance;

initialized once in the constructor.

Also check whether your code waits for both NAV_X and NAV_Y:

C++
if(!m_got_x || !m_got_y)
  return true;

That is fine, but if one of those variables is never received, the app will never publish.

7. Confirm Notify() is actually being called

Add a print immediately before Notify():

C++
std::cout << "Publishing ODOMETRY_DIST = "
          << m_total_distance << std::endl;

Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);

Then watch in another terminal:

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST

If your app prints Publishing ODOMETRY_DIST = ... but uXMS does not show it, check that uXMS is connected to the same MOOSDB port and community.

For example, if your .moos file uses:

moos
ServerHost = localhost
ServerPort = 9000

then launch:

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST --serverhost=localhost --serverport=9000

or use the same mission file if applicable:

Bash
uXMS targ_shoreside.moos ODOMETRY_DIST
8. Make sure Notify() uses the exact variable name

This must match exactly:

C++
Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);

Watch exactly the same name:

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST

These are different variables:

C++
ODOMETRY_DIST
ODOMETRY_DISTANCE
Odometry_Dist
odometry_dist
ODOMETRY DIST

MOOS variable names are case-sensitive.

9. Use uPokeDB to isolate whether the problem is your app or uXMS

Try directly poking the output variable:

Bash
uPokeDB ODOMETRY_DIST=123.45

Then watch:

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST

If uXMS still does not show it, your problem is not the app. It is probably that uXMS is connected to the wrong MOOSDB, wrong port, wrong community, or wrong mission file.

If uXMS does show 123.45, then uXMS is fine and your app is not publishing.

10. Minimal debugging checklist

Use this order:

Is the app connected?

Bash
uXMS DB_CLIENTS

Are input variables being published?

Bash
uXMS NAV_X NAV_Y

Did the app subscribe to them?

C++
Register("NAV_X", 0);
Register("NAV_Y", 0);

Does OnNewMail() receive them?

C++
std::cout << "Received mail: " << key << std::endl;

Is Iterate() running?

C++
std::cout << "Iterate running" << std::endl;

Does the logic reach Notify()?

C++
std::cout << "Publishing ODOMETRY_DIST = " << m_total_distance << std::endl;
Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);

Is uXMS connected to the same MOOSDB?

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST --serverhost=localhost --serverport=9000

The fastest practical test is: manually uPokeDB NAV_X and NAV_Y, print every mail item in OnNewMail(), print every Iterate(), and print immediately before Notify(). That tells you exactly where the chain breaks.

claude.text:
Great debugging question! Here's a systematic walkthrough for each layer:

1. Check your subscriptions in OnStartUp()

OnNewMail() only fires if you actually registered for the variables you need. Make sure you have:

cpp
bool pOdometry::OnStartUp() {
    AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp();
    Register("NAV_X", 0);
    Register("NAV_Y", 0);
    return true;
}

Common mistake: calling Register() before AppCastingMOOSApp::OnStartUp() — the base class can wipe registrations if called afterward.

2. Verify OnNewMail() is actually firing

Add a temporary cout or reportEvent():

cpp
bool pOdometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail) {
    AppCastingMOOSApp::OnNewMail(NewMail);
    for (auto &msg : NewMail) {
        cout << "GOT MAIL: " << msg.GetKey() << " = " << msg.GetDouble() << endl;
        if (msg.GetKey() == "NAV_X") m_nav_x = msg.GetDouble();
        if (msg.GetKey() == "NAV_Y") m_nav_y = msg.GetDouble();
    }
    return true;
}

If you see nothing printed, the upstream publisher (pMarinePID, iGPS, etc.) may not be running, or the variable names are mismatched — double-check with uXMS or uMS to confirm NAV_X/NAV_Y are actually being published.

3. Check AppTick in your .moos block

Iterate() runs at the rate set by AppTick. If it's missing or zero, Iterate() never fires:

ProcessConfig = pOdometry
{
    AppTick   = 10   // Hz — make sure this is nonzero
    CommsTick = 10
}

Add a sanity print in Iterate() to confirm it's being called at all:

cpp
bool pOdometry::Iterate() {
    AppCastingMOOSApp::Iterate();
    cout << "Iterate() called, dist so far: " << m_total_dist << endl;
    // ... your distance calculation ...
    Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_dist);
    return true;
}
4. Verify your distance logic has valid first-point handling

A very common bug: on the first iteration you haven't received a previous (x, y) yet, so you compute a garbage distance. Guard against this:

cpp
bool pOdometry::Iterate() {
    AppCastingMOOSApp::Iterate();

    if (!m_first_reading) {
        double dx = m_nav_x - m_prev_x;
        double dy = m_nav_y - m_prev_y;
        m_total_dist += sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy);
    }

    m_prev_x = m_nav_x;
    m_prev_y = m_nav_y;
    m_first_reading = false;

    Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_dist);
    return true;
}

Without the m_first_reading guard, the first call subtracts uninitialized m_prev_x/y (likely 0,0) from whatever the vehicle's starting coordinates are, spiking your odometry.

5. Confirm Notify() is correct

Notify() takes the exact variable name as a string — a typo silently publishes under the wrong name:

cpp
Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_dist);  // correct
Notify("ODOMOTRY_DIST", m_total_dist);  // silent failure — typo!

Also make sure you're not calling Notify() from inside OnNewMail() — it should live in Iterate().

6. Watch it live in uXMS

Once you think it's working, in a separate terminal:

bash
uXMS targ_shoreside.moos   # or whatever your mission file is

Then scope on your variable:

ODOMETRY_DIST
NAV_X
NAV_Y

If NAV_X/NAV_Y are updating but ODOMETRY_DIST is not appearing at all, the Notify() line is never being reached. If it appears but stays at 0, your mail is not being parsed correctly.

Debugging checklist summary
Layer	What to check
Subscriptions	Register("NAV_X") and Register("NAV_Y") called in OnStartUp()
Mail receipt	Print in OnNewMail() loop to confirm messages arrive
AppTick	.moos block has AppTick = N where N > 0
Iterate firing	Print at top of Iterate() to confirm it runs
First-reading guard	Don't accumulate distance on iteration 1
Notify spelling	Exact string match, called from Iterate() not OnNewMail()

gemini.text:
When a MOOS variable isn't hitting the community, it usually means there is a disconnect between the incoming data, the application loop, or the publication call itself. Since you are using a pOdometry-style application, the issue typically lies in how your mail drives your distance calculations.

Here is a systematic checklist to debug your MOOS app’s internal logic using uXMS and standard MOOS logging.

1. Verify Subscriptions in OnConnectToServer()

If your app isn't calculating distance, it might not be receiving the raw positioning data (like NAV_X and NAV_Y) to begin with.

The Check: Ensure your variables are explicitly registered inside OnConnectToServer(), not just OnInit(). If the connection drops and reconnects, OnConnectToServer() is what re-establishes the registry.

Code Example:

C++
bool COdometry::OnConnectToServer() {
    DoRegistrations();
    return true;
}

void COdometry::DoRegistrations() {
    m_Comms.Register("NAV_X", 0);
    m_Comms.Register("NAV_Y", 0);
}


uXMS Debugging: Run uXMS AppName and press r to see the app's current subscriptions. If NAV_X and NAV_Y aren't listed as subscriptions, your app is blind to incoming data.

2. Check OnNewMail() Execution

If the subscriptions are correct, ensure the mail is actually arriving and being parsed.

The Check: Add MOOSTrace statements to see if OnNewMail() is firing when NAV_X or NAV_Y changes.

Code Example:

C++
bool COdometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail) {
    MOOSMSG_LIST::iterator p;
    for(p = NewMail.begin(); p != NewMail.end(); p++) {
        CMOOSMsg &msg = *p;
        std::string key = msg.GetKey();

        if (key == "NAV_X") {
            m_current_x = msg.GetDouble();
            m_nav_x_received = true; 
        } else if (key == "NAV_Y") {
            m_current_y = msg.GetDouble();
            m_nav_y_received = true;
        }
    }
    return true;
}


uXMS Debugging: Check the uXMS source column for NAV_X. If its write-time isn't updating, no other app is publishing the data your app needs.

3. Validate Iterate() and AppTick

MOOS apps calculate and publish inside Iterate(). If Iterate() isn't running, or is exiting early, Notify() will never be called.

The Check: 1. Ensure your AppTick is set to a non-zero value in the .moos configuration file (e.g., AppTick = 4 or 10). If it is 0, Iterate() will never execute.
2. Guard your calculation logic. If you are calculating distance using d=
Δx
2
+Δy
2
	​

, you must ensure you have received at least one valid initial reading so you don't calculate an astronomical distance from (0,0).

Code Example:

C++
bool COdometry::Iterate() {
    // Don't calculate until we have at least one valid measurement pair
    if (!m_nav_x_received || !m_nav_y_received) return true; 

    if (m_first_reading) {
        m_previous_x = m_current_x;
        m_previous_y = m_current_y;
        m_first_reading = false;
        return true;
    }

    // Calculate distance
    double dx = m_current_x - m_previous_x;
    double dy = m_current_y - m_previous_y;
    double step_dist = sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy);

    m_total_distance += step_dist;

    // Update previous states
    m_previous_x = m_current_x;
    m_previous_y = m_current_y;

    // Publish
    m_Comms.Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);
    return true;
}

4. Is Notify() Actually Publishing?

If your logic passes but ODOMETRY_DIST still doesn't appear, ensure Notify() isn't being skipped by an early return statement or a failing conditional block.

The Check: Add a quick debug print immediately next to the notify call to verify it's being reached:

C++
MOOSTrace("Publishing Distance: %f\n", m_total_distance);
m_Comms.Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_distance);


If you see the MOOSTrace printout in your terminal but nothing shows up in uXMS, it is highly likely that your application has disconnected from the MOOSDB or is connected under a different name than the one you are monitoring.

5. How to scope it with uXMS

To see exactly what is happening in real-time, open your terminal and run:

Bash
uXMS mission.moos ODOMETRY_DIST NAV_X NAV_Y


Toggle Content (c): Press c to see who is publishing what. Ensure your app's name shows up under the "src" column for ODOMETRY_DIST.

Toggle Virgins (v): By default, uXMS hides variables that have never been assigned a value. Press v to show un-pulsed variables. If ODOMETRY_DIST shows up empty, your app successfully registered it, but Notify() has not yet executed.

chatgpt_55_instant.text:
Debug it in this order:

Confirm the app is running
In uXMS, watch:

APPCAST
DB_CLIENTS
PROC_WATCH_EVENT

Make sure your app actually launched and is connected to the MOOSDB.

Confirm subscriptions happen in OnConnectToServer()
Do not only register in OnStartUp(). Re-register after reconnects:

C++
bool Odometry::OnConnectToServer()
{
  Register("NAV_X", 0);
  Register("NAV_Y", 0);
  return true;
}

bool Odometry::OnStartUp()
{
  OnConnectToServer();
  return true;
}

Check that input mail is arriving
In OnNewMail(), temporarily print or appcast every incoming key:

C++
for(auto& msg : NewMail) {
  std::cout << "MAIL: " << msg.GetKey()
            << " = " << msg.GetString() << std::endl;
}

Also watch in uXMS:

Bash
uXMS NAV_X NAV_Y

If NAV_X/NAV_Y are not changing, the bug is upstream, not in odometry.

Keep OnNewMail() for storing state only
A good pattern is:

C++
bool Odometry::OnNewMail(MOOSMSG_LIST &NewMail)
{
  for(auto& msg : NewMail) {
    std::string key = msg.GetKey();

    if(key == "NAV_X") {
      m_x = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_x = true;
    }
    else if(key == "NAV_Y") {
      m_y = msg.GetDouble();
      m_got_y = true;
    }
  }
  return true;
}

Do distance accumulation and publishing in Iterate()

C++
bool Odometry::Iterate()
{
  if(!m_got_x || !m_got_y)
    return true;

  if(!m_initialized) {
    m_prev_x = m_x;
    m_prev_y = m_y;
    m_initialized = true;
    return true;
  }

  double dx = m_x - m_prev_x;
  double dy = m_y - m_prev_y;

  m_total_dist += hypot(dx, dy);

  m_prev_x = m_x;
  m_prev_y = m_y;

  Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", m_total_dist);
  return true;
}

Check AppTick
If Iterate() publishes, then AppTick controls how often:

ProcessConfig = pOdometry
{
  AppTick   = 4
  CommsTick = 4
}

If AppTick is missing, typoed, or your app is not listed under pAntler, Iterate() may not be running as expected.

Watch the exact output variable
In uXMS, use the exact spelling:

Bash
uXMS ODOMETRY_DIST

Common mistakes:

C++
Notify("ODOM_DIST", m_total_dist);      // wrong name
Notify("ODOMETRY_DISTANCE", m_total);   // wrong name
Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", "12.3");        // string instead of double, usually okay but not ideal

Most likely failure chain: the app only registers in OnStartUp(), reconnects, stops receiving NAV_X/NAV_Y, Iterate() has no valid state, and Notify("ODOMETRY_DIST", ...) never fires.
